comment.narrative:
1. Provide hand tools and material support for 60,000 HHs who are involved in landscape management activities both at communal land and their own farmland;
2. Construct Soil and water conservation structures in 25, 000-hectare land in 12 woredas and Rehabilitate 5000 hectares of pastoral land to convert it to productive grazing and farmland by enclosing critically degraded land, by clearing invasive plants and planning fodder trees;
3. Provide inputs and technological support for 5,000 (3000 women & 2000 men) beneficiaries to reduce biomass fuel consumption;
4. provide input to 25,000 HHS to increase diversified agricultural products at landscape level;
5. Link 60 SHG groups to local financial institutions to facilitate credit services by providing in kind assets as business inputs;
6. Document project results on audiovisual and print production and dissemination to mobilize additional resource to ILM activities.

1. 21,427 hhs beneficiaries (14,154 M and 7,273 F) provided with farm tools for integrated land scape management activities;
2. Soil and water conservation structures constructed in 18,214ha in 10 woredas; 1905 ha of pastoral land converted to productive grazing and farmland by enclosing critically degraded land, by clearing invasive plants and planning fodder trees;
3. 5,708 (2,356 M and 3,352 F) households provided with inputs and have got technological support to reduce biomass fuel consumption;
4. 15, 895 hhs (9,245 M and 6650 F) provided with inputs to increase diversified agricultural products at landscape level;
5. 58 SHG with a total member of 921 (379 M and 542 F) are linked to local financial institutions. 3536 (2,261 M and 1,275 F) households benefited through small irrigation development.
6. 21 project results print production have been prepared and documented.
7. Gender mainstreaming action plans and decision support tools have been applied at 9 woredas and land scape level;

1. Multi-stakeholder and multi-scale platforms strengthened in 10 project sites/woredas to support integrated landscape management in food production practices; 
2. 30 environmental school clubs supported and make fully functional in school compounds; 
3. Gender-responsive-decision-support tools and participatory processes for INRM in food production practices in place in 9 project sites/woredas;
4. 32,411 ha addressed with improved soil and water management that also enhances biodiversity;
5. 28,952 ha under diversified production system and 1,855 ha of Agro-pastoral systems under integrated land management practice;
6. 56,688households with increased access to food through enhanced production and livelihoods diversification including off-farm activities;
7. Multi-scale ecosystem resilience, food security and GEBs in place at 9 project sites/ woredas and national levels; 
8. 8(Eight)value chain development incentive mechanisms/infrastructures supported;
9. An estimated amount of $84,000 investment for Integrated and Sustainable Landscape Management system at 10 project woredas;
10. Small scale irrigation created and accessed to 6,400 hhs while the plan was to access for 4,000 hhs.
